WEBVTT

00:00:09,070 --> 00:00:14,830
I have no family history of cancer, but since I'm under 50 and have my lateral breast cancer,

00:00:14,830 --> 00:00:22,270
they suggest I do genetic testing. Is it information worth having or not?

00:00:23,390 --> 00:00:29,790
So the genetic testing that the oncology department is going to do is going to be for some very

00:00:29,790 --> 00:00:36,750
specific genes that can have tendencies to run into family that could be at cause of cancer.

00:00:37,310 --> 00:00:44,990
Now they can do some genes that we can't do. Now there are genes that they will typically run

00:00:44,990 --> 00:00:49,150
when they do that study. And what are the bronchagines? So this person says I was always under

00:00:49,150 --> 00:00:54,830
the impression that even if they found the bronchagines or other mutations, that should not really

00:00:54,830 --> 00:01:00,670
change my treatment decisions. Or would it be helpful to know if there are any other cancer related

00:01:00,670 --> 00:01:07,230
genes lurking in my DNA? My answer to this is if they're willing to run this test and your

00:01:07,230 --> 00:01:12,030
insurance is going to cover it by all means, I'd say go for it. Yes, run the gene, run the test.

00:01:12,670 --> 00:01:18,190
Now when we we also look in our genetic profile, we look at your bronchagines too. Now there are

00:01:19,310 --> 00:01:25,790
bronchagines, all your genes have family of genes. So your bronchagines are a family of genes.

00:01:25,790 --> 00:01:33,630
There's actually about around 100 different bronchagines. Now there's really only when they do a test

00:01:33,630 --> 00:01:38,110
for you, when the oncologist does a test, they're really looking at about three or four of the bronchagines.

00:01:38,110 --> 00:01:49,150
Because those are have been tied to the most significant ones that can lead to an inability to fight

00:01:49,230 --> 00:01:55,310
cancer. So I just said your bronchagines are tumor suppressor genes. There are numerous

00:01:55,310 --> 00:02:01,950
different families of tumor suppressor genes like the bronchagines that if you have mutations,

00:02:01,950 --> 00:02:08,830
slash defects of those genes, those genes won't work as well. Why is that important? Because tumor

00:02:08,830 --> 00:02:17,950
suppressor genes turn on when a cell goes into rapid replication and it turns on and it stimulates

00:02:18,590 --> 00:02:26,270
apoptosis of the cell. So it can turn on and stimulate cell death so that that's one way that

00:02:26,270 --> 00:02:31,870
God put in us to be able to fight against cancer. Because remember we've said, what are the struggles

00:02:31,870 --> 00:02:39,150
with cancer? Is that cancer? Is your own cells? Something gets inside the cell, damages the DNA, causes

00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:44,510
the DNA to go into rapid replication. What could it be? Could it be a toxin? Could be a mold toxin?

00:02:44,510 --> 00:02:51,070
Could be a pesticide? Could be an herbicide? Could be a heavy metal? Could be a bacteria? Could

00:02:51,070 --> 00:02:58,910
be a lime bacteria? Could be a fungus? Could be a virus? Something gets inside the cell and causes

00:02:58,910 --> 00:03:05,870
the cell to go into rapid replication and then it replicates cells that are also equally dysfunctioning

00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:13,790
in a state of rapid replication. That's what cancer is. Where that happens, you have lots of different

00:03:13,790 --> 00:03:20,990
tumor suppressor genes. Right now there's known about 25 different families of tumor suppressor

00:03:20,990 --> 00:03:30,510
genes that will turn on and help cause that cell to go to die. It's an internal stimulation of

00:03:30,510 --> 00:03:38,430
in what's called an intrinsic apoptotic pathway. It causes that cell to die. If you have defects in those

00:03:38,430 --> 00:03:45,710
genes, you can have a more difficult time killing cancer because that you're completely

00:03:45,710 --> 00:03:53,790
reliant on the external pathway or the extrinsic apoptotic pathway. Something killing the cell from

00:03:53,790 --> 00:03:57,870
the outside, your immune system, killing the cell from the outside. That's what we're trying to do

00:03:57,870 --> 00:04:02,670
with the right machines. Stimulate in a viewed response on the cell to kill the cell from the

00:04:02,670 --> 00:04:11,070
outside. Stimulated extrinsic apoptotic pathway. That's what we're trying to do with nutrition. That's

00:04:11,070 --> 00:04:14,270
what you're trying to do with dietary, that's what you're trying to do with everything else to try

00:04:14,270 --> 00:04:21,150
to do with chemotherapy. Stimulate that extrinsic pathway. Well, your body has this intrinsic pathway

00:04:21,790 --> 00:04:26,350
through the tumor suppressor genes. But when we have defects on those genes, we have a more

00:04:26,350 --> 00:04:34,510
difficult time killing cancer through that pathway. It doesn't cause cancer. If you have a

00:04:34,510 --> 00:04:46,110
brachogy defect, does that cause cancer? That's not what tumor suppressor genes do. But if you do

00:04:46,110 --> 00:04:51,870
get cancer, you'll have a more difficult time killing it simply because of that. But understand,

00:04:51,870 --> 00:04:58,510
there's even more widely studied than the brachogynes. There are the P53 genes. There's the

00:04:58,510 --> 00:05:06,510
K-ROS genes. There's tons of different gene families that I look at when I look at your genetics

00:05:06,510 --> 00:05:13,390
and that's how I make different recommendations based upon that. But long story short, if they're really

00:05:13,390 --> 00:05:24,430
doing that test for you, by all means, I'm all for testing. It's just a blood sample. Let them do it.

